(2267259, 305510, 42125, '<td valign="top">OK, I was wrong, the picture is good though.<br/>Love the colours!<br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Gunnsi:</b></div><hr/><i> Iceland ?, wild guess! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>P.S. I have made a few guesses that were right :-)</td>', 'OK, I was wrong, the picture is good though.Love the colours!Originally posted by Gunnsi: Iceland ?, wild guess!   P.S. I have made a few guesses that were right :-)', '2006-03-28 18:49:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(2267782, 306780, 19978, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>by strangeghost<br/> <br/><i>The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.</i> <br/> <br/><b>TECHNIQUE</b> <br/>The first thing that strikes me about this image, and it''s like an iron upside the head, are the horribly blown out sky highlights. Everything else looks a little underexposed (maybe auto mode?) but the powerfully overexposed whites are just too in-your-face. <br/> <br/><b>COMPOSITION</b> <br/>It''s really an odd looking composition, and doesn''t come off that well, IMO. Meets the challenge, yes. Footware is prominently featured, but the angle of the tree is very odd, the left shoe is cut off (bad when that shoe is the subject) and we''re seeing only the bottoms of the feet. I believe that this idea has some potential. A person - especially a kid - climbing a tree is a very interesting subject, but without more of that person to work with in the image, it''s hard to hold the interest. I like the way the legs are evidently wrapped around that branch, because it suggests some tension and drama. However, it''s difficult to tell if that''s what''s going on or not. Most of the frame is dominated by the inert mass of the trunk, which is doing nothing for your composition. Take this composition, and move to the right about two feet so the feet (and maybe a little more of the body) are more centered in the frame, and make it a little easier for us to relate to the climber. Just an idea. Many other possibilities occur to me as I look at this photo.<br/> <br/><b>EMOTIONAL IMPACT</b> <br/>It''s very minimal impact results from the technical shortcomings and questionable composition. More attention to those two areas probably would have saved this from being in the sleeper section of a challenge.<br/> <br/><b>FINAL THOUGHTS</b> <br/>Your comments captured some of the things I was getting at, and your final score of 4.47 is about what I would have predicted. I should also add that I found your lack of a photographer''s comment or any technical details about your photo frustrating (shutter, aperture, etc.). When you ask for a critique, you should consider sharing some of your own thoughts about your work. What were you aiming for? What did you like or not like about the photo? What were your frustrations with your composition, etc.? It can help your CC''er understand better your intent and purpose in make the image you did.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club by strangeghost The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.  TECHNIQUE The first thing that strikes me about this image, and it''s like an iron upside the head, are the horribly blown out sky highlights. Everything else looks a little underexposed (maybe auto mode?) but the powerfully overexposed whites are just too in-your-face.  COMPOSITION It''s really an odd looking composition, and doesn''t come off that well, IMO. Meets the challenge, yes. Footware is prominently featured, but the angle of the tree is very odd, the left shoe is cut off (bad when that shoe is the subject) and we''re seeing only the bottoms of the feet. I believe that this idea has some potential. A person - especially a kid - climbing a tree is a very interesting subject, but without more of that person to work with in the image, it''s hard to hold the interest. I like the way the legs are evidently wrapped around that branch, because it suggests some tension and drama. However, it''s difficult to tell if that''s what''s going on or not. Most of the frame is dominated by the inert mass of the trunk, which is doing nothing for your composition. Take this composition, and move to the right about two feet so the feet (and maybe a little more of the body) are more centered in the frame, and make it a little easier for us to relate to the climber. Just an idea. Many other possibilities occur to me as I look at this photo. EMOTIONAL IMPACT It''s very minimal impact results from the technical shortcomings and questionable composition. More attention to those two areas probably would have saved this from being in the sleeper section of a challenge. FINAL THOUGHTS Your comments captured some of the things I was getting at, and your final score of 4.47 is about what I would have predicted. I should also add that I found your lack of a photographer''s comment or any technical details about your photo frustrating (shutter, aperture, etc.). When you ask for a critique, you should consider sharing some of your own thoughts about your work. What were you aiming for? What did you like or not like about the photo? What were your frustrations with your composition, etc.? It can help your CC''er understand better your intent and purpose in make the image you did.', '2006-03-28 22:41:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(2267844, 305286, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Rustic Chic" is a nice addition into the footwear challenge.  You have defiantly meet the qualification of the challenge.  <br/> <br/>The image is a very nice one.  The wind blowing through her hair is a nice touch.  I really like that you choose to shot outdoors to enhance the rustic/nature look.  I think the image probably lost some points because the footwear really wasn''t the focus, I personally think of the girl as the subject and the shoes a nice accessory.   The lighting, gently falling on her is a nice balance for a soft subject.  You might try playing with the contrast to enhance the colors.  This would probably make the overall image a little stronger.  One thing I have noticed over a few challenge is that the bright or vibrant colored image score higher.  I personally like the composition for a portrait but not so much for the challenge.  <br/> <br/>You defiantly a very good first entry, I look forward to your future entries. After the first challenge it gets easier and I think  you are off to a great start!  <br/></td>', '*Critique Club* "Rustic Chic" is a nice addition into the footwear challenge.  You have defiantly meet the qualification of the challenge.   The image is a very nice one.  The wind blowing through her hair is a nice touch.  I really like that you choose to shot outdoors to enhance the rustic/nature look.  I think the image probably lost some points because the footwear really wasn''t the focus, I personally think of the girl as the subject and the shoes a nice accessory.   The lighting, gently falling on her is a nice balance for a soft subject.  You might try playing with the contrast to enhance the colors.  This would probably make the overall image a little stronger.  One thing I have noticed over a few challenge is that the bright or vibrant colored image score higher.  I personally like the composition for a portrait but not so much for the challenge.   You defiantly a very good first entry, I look forward to your future entries. After the first challenge it gets easier and I think  you are off to a great start!  ', '2006-03-28 23:06:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2267941, 304846, 43434, '<td valign="top">Hi From the critique club.<br/> <br/>Sorry I am writing this from work so no template to use.<br/> <br/>First impression<br/> <br/>This hits on a couple of different perspecitves - but if it is the boot in the stirrup I am meant to focus on I can not make it out clearly ... I am concerned that the black and white is overkill - but others don''t seem to have mentioned this.<br/> <br/>Subject<br/> <br/>Meets the challenge - but as mentioned i think is a little on the dark side:)<br/> <br/>Composition<br/> <br/>Good this I really like ... cropping os the main of the beast is on thirds ... cool idea.<br/> <br/>Brightness and technical stuff<br/> <br/>I am not sure ... I like natural light .. always have .. but in this picture the boot itself is lost ...and I think the reason is the black adn white ... the contrasts in this don''t make the boot pop - like it would against the natural colour of the horse.<br/> <br/>Final note<br/> <br/>I think this is cool.  You did good and I know are going on to better and better pics.<br/> <br/>Private message me if you wish any clarifications.<br/> <br/>Cheeers<br/>Kari</td>', 'Hi From the critique club. Sorry I am writing this from work so no template to use. First impression This hits on a couple of different perspecitves - but if it is the boot in the stirrup I am meant to focus on I can not make it out clearly ... I am concerned that the black and white is overkill - but others don''t seem to have mentioned this. Subject Meets the challenge - but as mentioned i think is a little on the dark side:) Composition Good this I really like ... cropping os the main of the beast is on thirds ... cool idea. Brightness and technical stuff I am not sure ... I like natural light .. always have .. but in this picture the boot itself is lost ...and I think the reason is the black adn white ... the contrasts in this don''t make the boot pop - like it would against the natural colour of the horse. Final note I think this is cool.  You did good and I know are going on to better and better pics. Private message me if you wish any clarifications. CheeersKari', '2006-03-28 23:49:17.000000', NULL, 0),
